<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Diego</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1474">1474</a>–1548</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Juan Diego Cuauhtlatoatzin, Saint Juan Diego</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Diego was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> convert to whom, according to Catholic tradition, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a> appeared on the hill of Tepeyac near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in December 1531. The image said to have been imprinted on his cloak became the focus of the Guadalupe devotion. He was canonised by Pope John Paul II in 2002 as the first indigenous saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-diego.jpg" alt="Juan Diego" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Cabrera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MIguel_Cabrera_-_Fiel_retrato_do_vener%C3%A1vel_Juan_Diego.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
